WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.200
Aubrey Plaza's career is on fire now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.560
but a medical setback nearly 20 years ago&nbsp;
threatened to end it before it ever began.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.560
In 2004, while she was a 20 year old undergraduate&nbsp;
at New York University, Aubrey Plaza suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.400
a stroke that left her temporarily&nbsp;
paralyzed, without the ability to speak.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.400
In a 2016 interview with the Guardian, Plaza&nbsp;
shared that she'd suffered from aphasia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.040
a disorder which can severely impact one's&nbsp;
ability to communicate. She explained,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.480
"I had expressive aphasia, where I&nbsp;
could understand what's happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.040
but I couldn't talk or communicate.&nbsp;
Like, you could say something and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.520
would know what you meant but I couldn't&nbsp;
express it or even write it. [...] When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.935
they gave me a piece of paper and a pen I&nbsp;
just kept writing lines instead of words."

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:45.280
"And every time they asked me how old I was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.960
I would go, 'Sixteen,' which was the only&nbsp;
number I could say. I don't know why."

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.080
Plaza added that immediately after&nbsp;
having the stroke — which happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.280
when either blood is prevented from&nbsp;
getting to the brain or a blood vessel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.040
within the brain itself bursts —&nbsp;
she couldn't walk. She recalled,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.600
"When it first happened to me I was paralyzed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.800
but I was so young that my brain healed itself&nbsp;
really fast. I was really lucky in that way."

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.440
Maybe the most frightening part of the entire&nbsp;
ordeal, as Plaza explained to Fresh Air in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.320
is that, because she appeared okay when she got&nbsp;
to the emergency room, she wasn't examined right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.840
away. However, when she was, her condition&nbsp;
was immediately apparent. As she remembered,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.920
"A doctor finally examined me, and I&nbsp;
believe she asked me to put my right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.400
hand on my left knee. And I couldn't&nbsp;
do it. I was confused about right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.880
left. And I think that's when everyone&nbsp;
realized, oh, like, she had a stroke."

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.680
Plaza has been remarkably&nbsp;
open about what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.480
which is helpful to other stroke victims who may&nbsp;
be reluctant to talk about their experience for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:51.760
fear of being stigmatized. Strokes are, in fact,&nbsp;
distressingly common. As Plaza told Fresh Air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.800
she was visiting a friend at their apartment.&nbsp;
They were discussing a Hilary Duff concert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.400
she'd attended with her sister, when she&nbsp;
blacked out mid-sentence. When she came to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.120
she couldn't speak. Though her friends&nbsp;
thought she was joking at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.080
they eventually realized that it was serious&nbsp;
and called an ambulance. Plaza told Fresh Air,

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.000
"There was no recovery. I&nbsp;
mean when you have a stroke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.640
you have a stroke. There's nothing you can&nbsp;
do about it. Your brain has to heal itself."

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.720
Plaza added that she still has lingering&nbsp;
complications from the experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.360
even after cognitive therapy and a&nbsp;
successful recovery. As she explained,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.960
"But I still have [...] things&nbsp;
that only I would notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.200
are kind of [...] left over from&nbsp;
that incident. And since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.080
I've had some minor — they call them TIAs,&nbsp;
which are transient ischemic attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.960
that are tiny little strokes. So something's&nbsp;
up with my blood, but I don't know what it is."

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.240
Just a few years after her&nbsp;
devastating medical setback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.840
Plaza landed her career-defining role. In 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:54.400
she introduced the world to sullen intern April&nbsp;
Ludgate on the NBC sitcom Parks and Recreation.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.440
"April Ludgate, professional drinker."

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.800
Despite a slow start, the show&nbsp;
hit its stride in Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.440
and Plaza's hilariously deadpan&nbsp;
April was a huge part of its success.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:11.120
Alongside an all-star cast that included Amy&nbsp;
Poehler, Chris Pratt, Aziz Ansari, Adam Scott, Rob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:17.640
Lowe, Rashida Jones, Jim O'Heir, Retta, and Nick&nbsp;
Offerman, Plaza helped make Parks and Recreation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.280
one of the most beloved shows of the 2010s.&nbsp;
And despite its consistently low ratings, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.680
show's following and the love it got from critics&nbsp;
helped it run for a solid seven seasons. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.720
the best was yet to come for Plaza, who didn't let&nbsp;
her medical situation keep her from her acting.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.040
Plaza's career remained healthy after&nbsp;
Parks and Recreation. She appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.120
films like Mike and Dave Need Wedding Dates&nbsp;
and Ingrid Goes West as well as the small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.720
screen series Legion. And in 2022, she&nbsp;
embarked on an entirely new creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.120
journey with Season 2 of Mike White's hit&nbsp;
anthology series The White Lotus. White&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.880
Lotus allowed the actor to flex her muscles&nbsp;
as a performer and ultimately got attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.840
from the Television Academy in the form of&nbsp;
a supporting actress Emmy nomination. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.354
scary medical history or not, Plaza shows&nbsp;
no signs of slowing down anytime soon.

